{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1174659-cryptopolies-labyrinths","id":1174659,"title":"Cryptopolies & Labyrinths","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-01-09T14:39:56-08:00","id":1174659,"title":"Cryptopolies & Labyrinths","content":"I've been trying to figure out how these groups would be shaped and how to use them in a Chronicle, including possible friction between the two and now I am asking for thoughts and examples others have used.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-09T14:59:29-08:00","id":1174666,"title":"","content":"They're Mystery Cults, the only actual difference between them is that the Guardians use them for obfuscating truth while the Ladder uses them to try and prepare members for actual enlightenment. The Guardians and the Ladder attempt to infiltrate and take over each other's cults all the time. It's kind of similar to where the Guardians and the Mysterium get on each other's nerves, both hide away dangerous artifacts, but the Mysterium does it so only the worthy can study them while the Guardians do it to make sure it can never fall into the wrong hands (I.e. everyone else).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-09T16:57:04-08:00","id":1174694,"title":"","content":"I'm going to have to disagree with Mrm; saying they're both just Mystery Cults is a bit too reductionist.<br /> <br /> Cryptopolies are not just singular Mystery Cults but a network of cults and conspiracies, all of which have, at their essential core, some drive to help Sleepers attain some level of self-actualization. They aren't even necessarily Mystery Cults, though they typically will be. Some arms of a Cryptopoly, though, might simply be a self-interested conspiracy, ready to be mobilized to unknowingly serve the Awakened Nation when the time draws near.<br /> <br /> Cryptopoly cells are extremely varied. There might be a dozen or more of them, each with a different apparent public face, but all working to serve the agenda of their Awakened masters. They tend to contain elements of the Lesser Elemental Precepts, intended to make Lions out of lambs and Sages out of petty beasts. Ultimately, a Cryptopoly cell is more likely to feel like a cult with noble goals and dedication to higher ideals, and the Cryptopoly itself to feel like a stereotypical cultish conspiracy.<br /> <br /> The Labyrinth, on the other hand, doesn't just exist to hide secrets; they use it to hide secrets from the <i>unworthy</i>. When Guardians see someone they consider a worthy prospect, they tend to try and quietly initiate the person in the hopes they'll Awaken, just like every other Order. Labyrinths are occult traps, designed to lure in the curious and give them convincing but ultimately spurious occult lore to study. This doesn't just trap ignorant Sleepers, either; Sleepwalkers and the newly Awakened wander in, looking for answers, which is how most Awakened tend to end up joining the society of the Wise.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, you have a lot of leeway in shaping either. Cryptopolies, though, are used as tools by thearchs to manipulate society to their own ends, while Labyrinths are sacred duties for the Guardians. Thearchs might never actually even directly interact with the cults of their Cryptopoly; merely requesting its use when needed through the appropriate channels. Labyrinths, meanwhile, are much more personal for Guardians, who must always be on the look-out for potential.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-09T17:59:20-08:00","id":1174717,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2855_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2855\" data-vbnamecard=\"2855\">Axelgear</a> I'd posit you're not actually disagreeing with me, but expanding on what I said. Mystery Cults are simply the basic mechanical sense in which we can understand what a Labyrinth or Cryptopoly is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-10T19:18:49-08:00","id":1175061,"title":"","content":"I can definitely see the Guardians infiltrating a Ladder Cryptopoly to seek out potential candidates for Awakening that they think the Ladder would be better off losing. I can also kind of see the Ladder scrutinizing and acting against Guardian Labyrinths in hopes of rescuing people from an Exarch worthy dead end.<br /> <br /> Does anyone have examples of these cells, both Ladder and Guardian?<br /> How about some that have gone off the rails?<br /> <br /> \u200bI would also appreciate examples of how both orders go about maneuvering their potentials.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-12T05:06:47-08:00","id":1175426,"title":"","content":"An example Labirynth:<br /> <br /> For a glance, Hermes Corp seems like your typical multi-level marketing company. They deal mostly in kitchen appliances and cleaning supplies. They don't have a traditional distribution network - a person joining the business becomes an independent associate, trying to sell the product. You are rewarded not only with a cut of your profits, but also of the profits of the people you bring into the organization.<br /> <br /> The company code of conduct emphasises positive thinking and pursuit of success. Ruthlessness is encouraged when it helps the sales. In fact, it's even mandatory - the organization is quite famous from its low-quality wares and predatory behavior. Most of its lowest-tier members nearly bankrupt themselves, selling crappy kitchen appliances to their families and friends, or even buying them themselves to meet the quota. Some alienate people closest to them, trying to bring them into the fold. They end up penniless and bitter, laughingstocks in their circles of friends. The Guardians running the Labirynth don't pity them - they receive a valuable lesson after all.<br /> <br /> Sometimes, however, a person not fully buys into the corporate culture, but also manages to make a profit. They advance beyond the chaff, receiving public praise from their superiors. Soon they receive an evaluation - if they are found to be cutthroat enough, they are brought into the &quot;inner circle&quot;, where there are more opportunities to profit.<br /> <br /> Soon, a mentor appears, claiming to be impressed with the employee and willing to teach him certain techniques to improve their sales - for a price. It's mostly motivational screed filled with social science jargon (tailored to seem inspired) and some mysticism sprinkled on the top. Cultors observe their charges and make sure their first trials are successful to keep them interested. If they seem to be buying it, more &quot;techniques&quot; are doled out, more obviously magical than the first ones. They always cost something - if not money, then a favour for the Guardians. Of course, they are completely devoid of any Supernal truths.<br /> <br /> If at any point the charge shows the signs of potential Awakening, they are put in a more secure environment. A group of mages approaches them and tells them the company is a lie - a gauntlet meant to find people like them. They claim to represent the Lodge of Hermes, a group of people pursuing ancient knowledge for power. The cultist is initiated into the group and gets an access to the library filled with (fake) occult books. In the meantime, a group of Guardians do everything in their power to prevent their Awakening or mess it up when it happens.<br /> <br /> There are exceptions - some candidates join the organization and are disgusted by what they see. Most of them leave without a fuss. Some pretend to go with the flow, but the company is designed to weed them out. Some actually try to sabotage it from the inside - Guardians absolutely love this and try to induct them whenever it's possible.<br /> <br /> The local branches of Free Council and the Silver Ladder vehemently dislike the Labirynth. The former point out it looks creepily like a Mammon Ministry cult and grows fat by abusing Sleepers. The latter consider Hermes Corp ineffective - it punishes the Sheep for trying to aim higher, while the Lions' predatory behavior is encouraged. The Guardians retort that only those that buy into the cult's false ideology are harmed. It's a honey trap for the greedy and power-hungry, who would gladly serve the Exarchs if given a chance. Exploiting them provides them with a lesson and prevents them from being nabbed by the Seers. Denying the Exarchs their servants is nothing but moral, after all.<br /> <br /> (EDIT: I called a Labirynth a Cryptopoly...)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13425,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13425-gantolandon","user":"Gantolandon","date":". 01-12-2018, 08:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13425-gantolandon","name":"Gantolandon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"17"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-12T22:59:32-08:00","id":1175699,"title":"","content":"For some time now I've been pondering on the fact that the Mage the Awakening gameline itself would be a perfect model for a Labyrinth. People who might Awaken can be gently nudged into checking out this neat game that coincidentally mirrors the things they've been obsessing over lately while also not revealing anything that's True, effectively diverting their attention into fiction. It provides a level of false satiation, and wish fulfillment and comes with the bonus of tying into other mythos (Probably also Labyrinths) that a potential player can dig into with an eye to adapting it to the game rather than their reality. As a Mystery Cult it starts out at the bottom with people who have just been introduced to the game and are still asking all the newbie questions, to the intermediate levels where members are beginning to grasp the rules and form their own hypothesis, and probably at the high tiers are the devs. The sacred object they gather 'round, obviously, are the books. The only real flaw is that like with the Ancient Lands Pentalogy there's a risk that a potential Mage becomes so obsessed with the game and seeing everything in terms of the game that they manage to start seeing the Supernal despite the roadblocks and false information plants leading to them actually becoming a Mage. A Mage who manages to get to the end of this particular Labyrinth can also be particularly misinformed about Mage society or the workings of magic depending how much they leaned on the things in the books as truths.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 01-13-2018, 02:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-14T17:43:05-08:00","id":1176126,"title":"","content":"So, when Tom Hanks was prevented from jumping off the World Trade Center it caused his Awakening to fail, resulting in him getting stuck in the delusion that he was living in an Inn based off of a game?<br /> It seems like I read somewhere that the Guardians create crack pot groups with just enough of the truth to attract those leaning towards Awakening, but matched to lies enough to distract them away from further truth.<br /> \u200bIt might make for an interesting chronicle, or element there of, to have a group of Nameless who Awakened while playing such a Guardian created game or other deception, leading to conflicts with the Pentacle because the new group is laboring under a false paradigm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-14T17:44:45-08:00","id":1176127,"title":"","content":"Once a person succeeds in Awakening the Guardians typically come clean to avoid exactly that scenario.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}